<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zainal Abidin of Ternate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1500</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zainal Abidin of Ternate was ruler of Ternate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a> in the late fifteenth century. He studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> at Giri in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and is credited with formally adopting Islam and taking the title of sultan, making Ternate the first Islamic sultanate of the Moluccas.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zainal Abidin was born Tidore Wonge, also given as Gapi Buta, and is counted as the 18th or 19th ruler of Ternate. His life is known only from sources of the 16th century or later, which disagree on much. François Valentijn, writing in 1724, made him the son of Kolano Marhum, the eighteenth king, while other chronicles name his father as the seventeenth ruler Gapi Baguna II and his mother as a lady from the Sula Islands.</p><p>The oldest account, a Portuguese treatise on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> of about 1544, says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reached Maluku through Malay, Javanese and other merchants, and that a Muslim Javanese woman of high birth married King Tidore Wonge, who converted for her sake. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a> tell of a Raja of Maluku, probably the ruler of Ternate, who did homage to Sultan Alauddin Riayat Syah of Melaka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ternate was one of four traditional kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-maluku">North Maluku</a>, with Tidore, Bacan and Jailolo. According to Valentijn, the new ruler first faced attacks from Jailolo, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halmahera">Halmahera</a> and allied with Tidore, until peace was made in 1488. Around 1495 he travelled with his companion Hussein to Giri on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri">Sunan Giri</a> kept a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>, and there won the name Sultan Bualawa, the Sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">Cloves</a>.</p><p>On his return he replaced the royal title Kolano with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, brought the preacher Tuhubahahul from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and created a Bobato, or headman, to oversee Islamic law. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-tanah-hitu">Hikayat Tanah Hitu</a> instead says he was wounded in a fight at Bima on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> on the way home and died on board. His reign also saw the noble houses of Tomaitu and Tomagola carry Ternate's influence to the Sula Islands, Buru and Seram.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Valentijn gives his regnal years as 1486 to 1500, though their basis is unclear. His son Kaicil Leliatu succeeded him as the second sultan, Bayan Sirrullah. European observers noted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> long remained confined to the highest elite of Maluku, and its wider spread came only after 1570. The claims of Ternate to Buru, Seram, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a>, the Lease and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> rested partly on a pact he made with Pati Puti of Ambon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zainal Abidin of Ternate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Giri was a centre of Islamic learning that trained students from across the archipelago, including Sultan <b>Zainal Abidin of Ternate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/giri-kedaton">Giri Kedaton</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sultan Zainal Abidin</b>, who had studied Islam at Giri in Java, formally adopted Islam in the late 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
